  "title": "CHAPTER 3: TISSUES",
  "order": 3,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "sej4xjk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1 Introduction to Tissues"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Tissues are groups of similar cells performing a specific common function, allowing division of labor in multicellular organisms.",
        "Meristematic plant tissues (Apical, Intercalary, Lateral) actively divide to enable continuous growth.",
        "Simple permanent plant tissues comprise Parenchyma (storage), Collenchyma (flexibility), and Sclerenchyma (rigidity/strength).",
        "Complex permanent tissues comprise Xylem (water conduction) and Phloem (food translocation).",
        "Animal tissues are categorized into Epithelial (protection), Connective (support/binding), Muscular (movement), and Nervous (signal transmission)."
      ],
      "id": "whv63rj"
    },
    {
