    {
      "id": "mc3wda1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3 Classification of Tissues"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s4ay4tl",
      "content": "Tissues are broadly classified into:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "bkhyf0f",
      "content": "1. Plant Tissues"
    },
    {
      "id": "d1lklwp",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "vmo74cy",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_vmo74cy.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "gfrpkz5",
      "content": "2. Animal Tissues"
    },
    {
      "id": "p6s1s58",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4 .Plant Tissues"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ldeipyo",
      "content": "Plant tissues are divided into Meristematic and Permanent tissues."
    },
    {
      "id": "o5g47i1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "A. Meristematic Tissue"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rd8ijpb",
      "content": "Meristematic tissues consist of actively dividing cells. They are responsible for growth in plants."
    },
    {
      "id": "77j71cu",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Location</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Function</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Apical</p>",
            "<p>Root &amp; shoot tips</p>",
            "<p>Increase in length (Primary growth)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Intercalary</p>",
            "<p>Nodes / Internodes</p>",
            "<p>Elongation of internodes</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Lateral</p>",
            "<p>Cambium</p>",
            "<p>Increase in girth (Secondary growth)</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l8l8awk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "B. Permanent Tissue"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ubc4q8g",
      "content": "Permanent tissues are formed from meristematic tissues after losing their ability to divide."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tx93ken",
      "content": "They are of two types: Simple and Complex."
    },
    {
